1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,284
YA-NEC course, lesson 2: a 5-element VHF Yagi for 144 MHz. We model it in free space, optimize it, then check the lobes at 3 m above two different grounds.

2
00:00:11,334 --> 00:00:14,347
New: an empty model.

3
00:00:14,397 --> 00:00:24,905
Frequency 144.300 MHz, span 600 kHz: the weak-signal segment, 144.0 to 144.6 MHz.

4
00:00:24,955 --> 00:00:55,259
First the Variables tab: every dimension gets a name, as in 4nec2. LR, LDE, LD1-LD3 are the half-lengths of reflector, driven element and directors.

5
00:00:55,309 --> 00:01:20,297
XDE, XD1, XD2, XD3 are the positions along the boom, in metres from the reflector. Then Apply.

6
00:01:20,347 --> 00:01:29,164
Back to the 3D structure. Each element is a wire across Y; in the cells we type the variable names instead of numbers.

7
00:01:29,214 --> 00:01:54,495
Same for the driven element and the three directors: 6 mm tube (radius 3 mm), 21 segments each.

8
00:01:54,545 --> 00:01:59,095
Apply and recalculate: the five elements appear in 3D.

9
00:01:59,145 --> 00:02:06,603
Feed point: w2c, the centre of wire 2, the driven element.

10
00:02:06,653 --> 00:02:15,003
Starting design: 9.67 dBi, F/B 12.9 dB, but 47 - j37 ohm and SWR 2.09. Not good enough: let the optimizer work.

11
00:02:15,053 --> 00:02:21,936
Optimizer tab. On the left the goals: SWR, gain, F/B, each with its weight and threshold.

12
00:02:21,986 --> 00:02:28,603
'All elements' puts all nine variables in the list, each free to move within +/- 3 %.

13
00:02:28,653 --> 00:02:37,070
The frequencies to respect are the band edges and the centre: the antenna must be good across the whole segment.

14
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:44,139
Start.

15
00:02:44,189 --> 00:02:52,307
Done in a couple of seconds, 159 steps: gain 10.16 dBi, F/B from 12.9 to 23.3 dB, SWR 1.32 across the band.

16
00:02:52,357 --> 00:02:58,441
Apply to model: the new values go into the Variables.

17
00:02:58,491 --> 00:03:05,441
Free space, optimized: 37.9 + j0.2 ohm, 10.16 dBi, F/B 23.3 dB, beamwidth 54 x 71 degrees.

18
00:03:05,491 --> 00:03:09,002
Patterns in free space.

19
00:03:09,052 --> 00:03:17,569
Clean main lobe, small rear lobe: this is the antenna by itself, without the ground.

20
00:03:17,619 --> 00:03:29,197
Now at 3 m above Average ground (er 13, sigma 0.005): choose the ground and type the height.

21
00:03:29,247 --> 00:03:38,264
At 3 m (about 1.4 wavelengths): 15.60 dBi at 9.6 degrees of elevation. Ground reflection adds over 5 dB in the main lobe.

22
00:03:38,314 --> 00:03:41,828
Elevation pattern over Average ground.

23
00:03:41,878 --> 00:03:51,628
The main lobe is low, 9.6 degrees, and above it the ground creates other lobes: typical of an antenna more than one wavelength high.

24
00:03:51,678 --> 00:03:58,450
Second ground: Very good (er 20, sigma 0.0303). Same antenna, same height.

25
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:06,251
15.69 dBi at 9.6 degrees: almost identical. At 144 MHz and 3 m the type of ground changes very little.

26
00:04:06,301 --> 00:04:09,917
Elevation pattern over Very good ground.

27
00:04:09,967 --> 00:04:19,584
Same lobes: at VHF what counts is the height in wavelengths, not the soil. A 50 ohm match (hairpin or gamma) completes the design.

28
00:04:19,634 --> 00:04:25,584
Variables, optimizer, ground: the full workflow in a few minutes. YA-NEC is free: github.com/ic8tem/YA-NEC - 73 de IC8TEM
